    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the expression \\(1 + 6 \\cdot 2 - 3 + 5 \\cdot 4 \\div 2\\), we need to follow the order of operations, often remembered by the acronym PEMDAS (Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication and Division (from left to right), Addition and Subtraction (from left to right)).\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Multiplication and Division from left to right:**\n   - First, we perform the multiplication \\(6 \\cdot 2\\):\n     \\[\n     6 \\cdot 2 = 12\n     \\]\n   - Next, we perform the division \\(5 \\cdot 4 \\div 2\\):\n     \\[\n     5 \\cdot 4 = 20\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     20 \\div 2 = 10\n     \\]\n\n   So, the expression now becomes:\n   \\[\n   1 + 12 - 3 + 10\n   \\]\n\n2. **Addition and Subtraction from left to right:**\n   - We perform the addition \\(1 + 12\\):\n     \\[\n     1 + 12 = 13\n     \\]\n   - Next, we perform the subtraction \\(13 - 3\\):\n     \\[\n     13 - 3 = 10\n     \\]\n   - Finally, we perform the addition \\(10 + 10\\):\n     \\[\n     10 + 10 = 20\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression is \\(\\boxed{20}\\).",
